From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] api: no trust by the peer address for requests sent by pages
 of other sites

With panel authentication off (panel_auth_enabled = false; it is on by
default), is_authenticated() lets in every request from a loopback,
private, link-local or Tailscale peer address without a session. The
address is the one of the client, not of the page that made the
request. The panel rejects a POST, PUT, PATCH or DELETE whose Origin
(or Referer) names another host than Host, but a name of another site
that resolves to the router address after the page has loaded (DNS
rebinding) has Origin equal to Host: such a page in a browser of a LAN
client calls the panel API as same-origin, reads GET /api/backup (the
whole configuration), saves settings and stops the core.

A request is now not trusted by the peer address when the browser marks
it as sent by another site (Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site), when its Origin
is null or names another host and port than Host, or when Host is a
domain name outside the local names (an IP literal, localhost, a single
label, .lan, .local, .home.arpa, .internal, .ts.net). Such a request
needs a session, the agent token or the local IPC token, as one from a
public address. Requests without these headers (curl, scripts) and the
panel opened by an address or a local name are not affected. With panel
authentication on nothing changes.

New test: test_foreign_browser_request.

